 ## Overview
-This method accelerates the geometry-intensive portion of direct volume ray casting by offloading perspective calculations, gradient estimation, and volume data resampling to dedicated hardware units [S1]. It solves the bottleneck where software implementations spend up to 80% of computation time on these specific operations [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on general-purpose fragment shaders or 3D texture units, this approach uses specialized address generation and precalculated weight memories to achieve high throughput (26–36 million transformed locations per second) while maintaining 16-bit precision for density and gradient values [S1].
+This method accelerates the geometry-intensive stages of direct volume ray casting—specifically perspective transformation, gradient estimation, and volume data resampling—by offloading them to dedicated hardware logic within a geometry unit [S1]. It solves the bottleneck where software implementations consume up to 80% of computation time on these operations [S1]. It differs from sibling methods like [Hardware-Accelerated Volume Rendering via 3D Texturing](2799-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering.md) by using explicit address generation and precalculated interpolation weights in custom silicon rather than relying on general-purpose texture sampling units [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates within the VIRIM architecture, where a farm of parallel geometry units processes scanlines [S1, S2]. Each geometry unit handles the transformation of volume data from object space to the resampled space required for ray casting.
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** Ray starting point $\mathbf{o}$ and direction vector $\mathbf{d}$ for each scanline; volume dataset $V$; density lookup table (LUT).
+*   **Output:** Resampled density values $\rho$ and gradient components $\nabla \rho$ (X and Y) for sampling locations along the ray.
 
-### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Input:** Ray starting point and direction vector for a scanline; original volume dataset (voxel values); density lookup table (LUT).
-*   **Output:** Resampled density values and estimated X/Y gradient components for each sample location along the ray.
+**Data Structures:**
+*   **Weight Memory:** Size $256\text{K} \times 16$ bits. Stores precalculated interpolation weights. Addressed by three 6-bit fractional parts (one per coordinate) of the resampling location [S1].
+*   **Gradient Buffer:** Limits the maximal resampled dataset size to $512^3$ [S1].
+*   **Density LUT:** Maps raw voxel values to density values [S1].
+*   **Cache:** 1-entry cache for previously used data voxels [S1].
 
-### Data Structures and Memory Layout
-*   **Weight Memory:** Stores precalculated interpolation weights. Size is $256\text{K} \times 16$ bits [S1].
-*   **Addressing:** Weights are addressed by three 6-bit fractional parts, one for each coordinate (X, Y, Z) of the resampling location [S1].
-*   **Voxel Memory:** Accesses 8 neighbor voxels conflict-free. Uses Fast Page Mode, alternate memory bank read-out, and a 1-entry cache for previously used voxels [S1].
-*   **Controller:** Returns 0 for addresses outside the data cube boundaries [S1].
-*   **Precision:** All calculations (density and gradient) use 16-bit data resolution [S1].
+**Steps:**
+1.  **Ray Initialization:** For each scanline, store the ray starting point and the vector to the next resample location [S1].
+2.  **Address Generation:** Use hardware to generate sampling point positions. Round the X, Y, Z components of the sampling location to obtain integer addresses of the 8 neighbor voxels in the original dataset [S1].
+3.  **Data Access:** Access the 8 neighbor voxels conflict-free. The memory controller returns 0 for addresses outside the data cube [S1].
+4.  **Density Mapping:** Map raw voxel values to density values using the density LUT [S1].
+5.  **Resampling (Backward Mapping):** Perform weighted interpolation among the 8 voxel neighbors to compute the density value at the fractional sampling location. Interpolation weights are retrieved from the Weight Memory using the 6-bit fractional parts of the coordinates [S1].
+6.  **Gradient Estimation:** Estimate the X and Y components of the gradient using 20 Sobel filters applied to the interpolated density values [S1].
 
