 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** A single geometry unit can generate 26 to 36 million transformed locations per second using true 3D resampling [S1].
-*   **Memory Bandwidth:** The dedicated memory system achieves data access rates of up to 640 MBytes per second (calculated as 40MHz $\times$ 8 neighbors $\times$ 2 Bytes/voxel) [S1].
-*   **Complexity:** The method shifts complexity from CPU/GPU general-purpose cores to specialized hardware logic. The use of precalculated weights reduces interpolation cost to a memory lookup and multiply-accumulate operation [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** The maximum resampled dataset size is limited to $512^3$ by the gradient buffer size [S1]. The original dataset size is limited by the available ORAM/DRAM capacity (e.g., $256^3$ for 4Mbit ORAMs) [S1].
-*   **When to use:** Suitable for real-time visualization of biomedical data where high throughput is required and the volume size fits within the hardware memory constraints [S1].
+*   **Performance:** One geometry unit generates 26 to 36 million transformed locations per second [S1].
+*   **Memory Bandwidth:** Achieves up to 640 MBytes/second using commercial DRAMs, Fast Page Mode, alternate memory bank read-out, and a 1-entry cache [S1].
+*   **Memory Constraints:** Maximal original dataset size is $256^3$ for 16-bit voxels with 4Mbit ORAMs, or $512 \times 512 \times 256$ for 16Mbit DRAMs. The maximal resampled dataset size is limited to $512^3$ by the gradient buffer size [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** High throughput for geometry operations; conflict-free memory access; real-time reloadable LUTs and memories [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Fixed hardware functionality; limited by gradient buffer size for resampled data; potential artifacts from discrete weight resolution [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This method originates from the Heidelberg Raytracer and the VIRIM (Volume Rendering Image Machine) architecture developed in the mid-1990s [S1]. It was designed to address the high computational cost of geometry operations in volume rendering, which accounted for 80% of the processing time in software implementations of the time [S1]. The approach leverages custom hardware to perform perspective calculations, gradient estimation, and resampling in parallel, enabling real-time interactive volume rendering [S1, S2].
+This method was developed as part of the Heidelberg Raytracer and VIRIM (Volume Rendering Image Machine) architecture [S1]. It addresses the computational bottleneck of geometry operations, which consume 80% of computation time in software implementations [S1]. The approach uses special-purpose hardware for perspective calculations, gradient estimation, and resampling [S1].
 
